  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mombasa Integrated Food Security Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Catholic Archdiocese of Mombasa (CAM) provides services to People living with HIV and AIDS (PLWHA), Orphans and Vulnerable Children (OVC) including Young Single Mothers (YSM), guardians and religious leaders. The services offered include HIV prevention, psychological needs, social and economic strengthening, lobbying and advocacy initiatives and capacity building in an effort to mitigate the impact of HIV and AIDS. 

The project continues to successfully assess and register young mothers for various services and empower them in the following areas: HIV prevention, sexually transmitted diseases, nutrition and importance of child immunizations. They have been facilitated to form support groups for experience sharing and support of each other. Some of them are undertaking short courses to ensure self reliance, and reduced dependency on the project. CAM has trained religious leaders, and uses them for pastoral work and counselling of beneficiaries. 

The referral system between community and health facilities has been improved through the support of community volunteers. These have helped in supporting PLWHA to adhere to treatment and early treatment of opportunistic infection. A total of 3,823 new PLWHA and 5,062 OVC have been identified and are receiving support. Formation of support groups of PLWHA is encouraged and supported.

The project continues to register good progress in Projects coordination; monitoring and evaluation, technical backstopping, and sustainability approaches are now more effective.  Strengthened linkages and networking with other diocesan programmes is another notable success.  This include OVC supported by USAID PEPFAR, HBC/OVC programme with support from USAID - APHIA II, Community Based Home Care (CBHC) programme; Shelter of Hope (CBHC) in Taita District; Solidarity with Girl child in distress (SOLGIDI) focusing on prevention of HIV for girl children whose parents are  sex workers; and youth adventure unlimited, behaviour change communication and life skills; Solidarity of women in distress (SOLWODI) programme for women in sex business and Coast Interfaith Council of Clerics. These relationships continue to contribute hugely to the success of this project.

CAFOD plans to partner with Archdiocese of Mombasa in the next three years (April 2013 to March 2016) to implement an integrated food security programme (IFS).  A needs assessment to inform the IFS design and implementation methodology is scheduled to be conducted by June 2013.  It is anticipated that following the needs assessment peace building activities, livelihoods security and disaster risk reduction will be integrated in the ongoing HIV and AIDS work to facilitate a comprehensive response to the community&#x2019;s needs.</narrative>
  </description>
